-- title:
--   Continuous piecewise linear functions, $n$-order hinges, and hinging-hyperplane sums
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $n$ and work with real-valued functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$, identified with `Fin n → ℝ`. This file sets up the three notions used by the Wang–Sun representation theorem for continuous piecewise linear functions.
--
--   **Continuous piecewise linear functions.** `CPWL` is defined inductively as the sublattice of $\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}^n}$ generated by the affine maps: every affine $T : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ is `CPWL`, and the class is closed under the pointwise operations $f \vee g = \max(f,g)$ and $f \wedge g = \min(f,g)$. This lattice-theoretic presentation is equivalent to the usual geometric one — continuous and affine on each cell of a finite polyhedral subdivision of $\mathbb{R}^n$ — and it is the presentation that makes structural induction available.
--
--   **$n$-order hinges.** Following Wang and Sun, a hinge is indexed by one less than its number of affine arguments, so an $n$-order hinge on $\mathbb{R}^n$ takes $n+1$ of them: `IsHinge h` holds when there are a sign $\sigma \in \{+1,-1\}$ and affine maps $\ell_0, \dots, \ell_n$ with
--
--   $$h(x) \;=\; \sigma \cdot \max_{0 \le i \le n} \ell_i(x) \qquad (x \in \mathbb{R}^n).$$
--
--   **Hinging hyperplane sums.** `IsHH f` holds when $f$ is a finite sum $\sum_{k=1}^{K} h_k$ of $n$-order hinges. The Wang–Sun theorem asserts that every `CPWL` function is an `IsHH` function; these definitions are the vocabulary in which that theorem and its supporting lemmas are stated.
-- source:
--   Definitions transcribed verbatim from the preamble of the Prove2Me theorem WangSun.Main (theorem_id 63eec3c9-377a-4237-8fe6-f5d41ac2d0e2), whose natural-language statement attributes the result to S. Wang and X. Sun, Generalization of hinging hyperplanes, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 51 (2005), 4425-4431.

import Mathlib

open Finset

variable {n : ℕ}

/-- Continuous piecewise linear functions on `ℝⁿ`, presented as the sublattice of
`ℝⁿ → ℝ` generated by the affine maps. -/
inductive CPWL : ((Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) → Prop
  | affine (T : (Fin n → ℝ) →ᵃ[ℝ] ℝ) : CPWL ⇑T
  | sup {f g} (hf : CPWL f) (hg : CPWL g) : CPWL (f ⊔ g)
  | inf {f g} (hf : CPWL f) (hg : CPWL g) : CPWL (f ⊓ g)

/-- Wang and Sun index hinges by one less than the number of affine arguments, so an
`n`-order hinge on `ℝⁿ` takes `n + 1` of them. -/
def IsHinge (h : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ (σ : ℝ) (L : Fin (n + 1) → ((Fin n → ℝ) →ᵃ[ℝ] ℝ)),
    (σ = 1 ∨ σ = -1) ∧
      h = fun x => σ * ((univ : Finset (Fin (n + 1))).sup' univ_nonempty fun i => L i x)

/-- A finite sum of `n`-order hinges. -/
def IsHH (f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ (K : ℕ) (h : Fin K → ((Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ)), (∀ k, IsHinge (h k)) ∧ f = ∑ k, h k


